#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097612} :activity/id #uuid "71e9c379-9817-5da3-85f9-50847626fa5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050787 :topic/id #uuid "67094ea1-3fa3-574a-a9fb-47317689906e" :topic/math-academy-id 1043} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097798 :tutorial/content "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad y=2t-t^3, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$, as shown.\n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 1) = 3t^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y(t)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(2t - t^{3}) \\cdot 3t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}6t^{3} - 3t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6\\int_{0}^{1}t^{3}dt - 3\\int_{0}^{1}t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} - 3 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 6(\\frac{1}{4} - 0) - 3(\\frac{1}{6} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d0c890f-fbe0-5ded-948b-b94d13caac3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "786032d8-d66a-579e-8336-348ca609cfb8" :step/math-academy-id 2136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096538 :question/id #uuid "85879186-0866-5159-905b-65d043681231" :question/math-academy-id "e-604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527964}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "296a64b2-a271-5186-bf37-e47d7b56624c" :step/math-academy-id 2138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096561 :question/id #uuid "95cdc31d-c3f2-50b6-9447-b7317af775f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7412"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097134}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "3a032caf-6330-5e24-bafb-d45c348ad375" :step/math-academy-id 27495 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097915 :tutorial/content "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! To see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0$. However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$, and we have\n$y\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "89e96b74-e406-52f5-b64e-46267df927e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7915 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "97757635-74ae-5269-ac75-2ea85021adb9" :step/math-academy-id 45599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097653}}] :activity/title "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096851}]}
